//! Pinned per-operation fuel bands: the calibration leg's committed output.
//!
//! [`BANDS`] is generated by `bin/calibrate` (which rewrites this file
//! atomically) and committed; the enforcement leg judges against these
//! constants and never refits. To re-pin — after a deliberate guest
//! toolchain bump, a kernel change, or a strategy change — run
//! `just fuzzfit-calibrate`, review the diff like a snapshot, and commit
//! with a dated movement annotation.
//!
//! The band key is **kernel × outcome**: an operation with a rejection arm
//! (`join`/`sync` on overlap, `without` on emptiness, `checked_sub` on
//! underflow) gets one band for its success path and one for its rejection
//! path. The two are different mechanisms with different cost laws — a
//! rejection can legitimately undercut the success line by decades (early
//! overlap detection), and pricing both from one pooled cloud lets a
//! superlinear regression in either arm hide under the other's ceiling.
//!
//! Band semantics: a step of kernel `k` with denominator `d` bits and fuel
//! `f` is in-band iff, writing `r = log₁₀ f − (intercept + slope · log₁₀ d)`,
//!
//! ```text
//! −(width_below + ENFORCE_MARGIN_BELOW) ≤ r ≤ width_above + ENFORCE_MARGIN
//! ```
//!
//! judged only for `d ≥ min_denom` (below the calibrated range the line
//! extrapolates the intercept's constant overhead downward, which
//! false-flags genuinely cheap small cases; the rare fresh draw above
//! `max_denom` is judged against the extrapolated line — but enforcement
//! shares the calibration leg's generators and budgets, so the catching
//! power lives *inside* the calibrated span, which is why the ceiling is
//! priced from the positive residuals alone and the reach families exist).
//! Below the fit floor the size-law bands hand off instead of going
//! silent: sub-floor steps of the [`SMALL_BAND_KERNELS`] — rumors'
//! bootstrap hot path — are judged against [`SMALL_BANDS`], one
//! constant-classified band per kernel over its own calibrated span (see
//! [`judge_small`]). Above-band is a regression flag; below-band is a
//! liveness flag (a dead fuel meter or an accidentally-constant kernel
//! reads below its own band, never silently green). The two widths are
//! priced separately because the residual cloud is one-sidedly heavy —
//! see [`crate::fit`].
//!
//! # The blessed-drift window
//!
//! The committed instruments bound how far a *uniform* fuel drift — every
//! step of one kernel costing `c×` its pinned law, sizes untouched — can
//! run before something reads red. The shape leg is structurally deaf to
//! it (uniform drift moves intercepts, never slopes), so the window is
//! set by the other two legs, per band key:
//!
//! ```text
//! window = min( 10^(width_above + ENFORCE_MARGIN − max deterministic-leg residual),
//!               10^(REFIT_TOLERANCE −/+ the key's honest prefix divergence) )
//! ```
//!
//! The first term is the point leg through the deterministic prefix: the
//! prefix sample already nearest the ceiling is the one a drift pushes
//! over first. The second is the staleness leg: drift lifts the prefix
//! refit's line decade for decade, and the key's honest pre-drift
//! divergence eats into (or, when the prefix sits below the pin, adds
//! to) the tolerance. Measured over the deterministic prefix at this
//! pin, most keys sit near the margin floor (~×1.6 in fuel), while the
//! window is widest exactly where calibration sampling is thinnest: a
//! ~×3 uniform drift passes all committed instruments on the thinnest
//! rejection arms (the sync and join overlap arms, whose few-hundred-
//! sample clouds carry the roster's widest ceilings), with every other
//! key under ~×2.5. Drift inside the window surfaces only at the next
//! deliberate re-pin, as diff the re-pinner must annotate; the
//! mechanism is unchanged by stating this — the window is the honest
//! price of margins that must also absorb allocator variance and honest
//! sampling dispersion.
//!
